  <h1>Proof and Correction Ledger</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">A correction does not merely alter a formula. It changes which later conclusions
    can still use that formula. This ledger follows five headline claims through the archive and stops each
    argument at the first step not supplied.</p>
</section>

{% include "partials/editorial-guide.njk" %}

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Audit rule</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-audit-rule">A proof must survive its own revisions</h2>
    <p class="lede">The archive deserves credit for recording errors. Consolidation must now propagate each
      correction forward instead of leaving the original theorem label in place.</p>
    <p>A numerical result can support a conjecture, test an implementation or expose a false generalisation.
      It cannot replace a missing uniform bound. A conditional Hadamard formula can describe what follows if
      every zero is on the critical line. It cannot be used as the unconditional starting point of an argument
      meant to prove a property without RH. A leading asymptotic can suggest a sign. It cannot determine a
      discrete sum unless the remainder is bounded more tightly than the proposed margin.</p>
    <p>This page uses “unproved” in that precise, local sense: the supplied documents do not close the claim.
      It does not assert that the claim is false, and it does not survey every result in the literature. Where
      the archive cites 2026 papers, their contents are treated as source reports until independently checked.
      <span class="hist">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>The audit also distinguishes a failed <em>bridge</em> from a failed <em>identity</em>. The corrected
      inverse-Laplace formula may be exact while the proposed positivity proof fails. The Cauchy&ndash;Schwarz
      proof on the real axis may be valid while its hoped-for continuation to the critical line is unavailable.
      Keeping the surviving half is the purpose of a ledger.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Source-forensic finding</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-which-positivity">Which earlier positivity result was actually proved?</h2>
    <p>The project did prove a universal finite-set Gram statement. For fixed \(\sigma\), the Rankin
      increment kernel has the form</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      D_{\kappa,\sigma}(x,y)=\kappa_{\rm R}\sum_{n\ge1}n^{-2\sigma-2}
      (e^{-ix\log n}-1)(e^{iy\log n}-1),
    \]</p>
    <p>and every finite quadratic form is a sum of squares:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \sum_{i,j}c_i\overline{c_j}D_{\kappa,\sigma}(x_i,x_j)
      =\kappa_{\rm R}\sum_{n\ge1}n^{-2\sigma-2}
      \left|\sum_i c_i(e^{-ix_i\log n}-1)\right|^2\ge0.
    \]</p>
    <p>This is unconditional and genuinely quantifies over every finite real point set. It is also a
      different kernel. Its spectrum consists of the frequencies \(\log n\) with positive Dirichlet weights.
      Suzuki’s arithmetic screw kernel is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      G_g(x,y)=\Psi(x)+\Psi(y)-\Psi(x-y).
    \]</p>
    <p>Changing the first kernel into the second requires the completed prime-power explicit formula. Möbius
      extraction is signed, and individual delayed prime-power hinges are not positive kernels. The exact
      Laplace conversion found by the project begins with the conditional clause “if \(G_g\) is positive”; it
      does not prove that clause.</p>
    <p>The older archive contains two other easy sources of confusion. Its real-axis theorem proves
      \((\log\xi)''(\sigma)>0\) using a positive \(\cosh\)-weighted measure, but the corresponding critical-line
      weight \(\cos(tu)\) is signed. Its heading “Global PSD” describes selected finite Galerkin matrices; the
      same master document says that extending their positivity to every cutoff, dimension and test function
      is open and equivalent to RH.</p>
    <p><strong>Verdict:</strong> an earlier universal positivity lemma exists, but restoring its exact object is
      decisive. No earlier lemma in the 52-file archive or the current programme proves
      \(\Psi(t)\ge0\) for every real \(t\), or \(G_g\succeq0\) for every finite real point set.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Seventh-stage operator audit</p>
    <h2 id="t-rh-double-mobius">Does the second inversion complete the Weil form?</h2>
    <p>Arithmetic primitive extraction and TTI’s geometric second turn are not the same operation. If
      \(M a=\mu*a\) extracts primitive divisor data and \(Z a=\mathbf1*a\) restores repetitions, then
      \(ZM=MZ=I\), while \(M^2\ne I\). The exact two-face model is instead</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      P=\begin{pmatrix}0&Z\\M&0\end{pmatrix},
      \qquad P^2=I.
    \]</p>
    <p>This is a genuine TTI-style involution: the first turn crosses from repetitions to primitives and the
      second returns. It does not create a new Weil metric. Its inverse face returns the all-integer ledger,
      and the positive metric making \(P\) isometric is only the original ledger norm transported to the
      primitive face.</p>
    <p>The adjoint candidate \(M^*GM\) is positive whenever \(G\) is positive, but that fact inherits its
      content from the chosen metric. With the ordinary feature norm it is unbounded. With the independently
      derived \(\kappa_{\rm R}/n^2\) Green metric it converges, yet its diagonal is smooth:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      D_{M,\kappa}(t,t)=C t^2+o(t^2).
    \]</p>
    <p>Suzuki’s target has a different local signature,</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      G_g(t,t)=2\Psi(t)=t\log(1/t)+O(t).
    \]</p>
    <p>The kernels therefore cannot be equal near the origin. Choosing a pullback metric designed to force
      equality would be circular unless TTI derived that metric before seeing the target. A successful route
      still needs an independent residue or history-bearing cocycle \(B\) with
      \(W(f*\widetilde f)=\lVert Bf\rVert^2\) on the full Weil test space. The exact operator, numerical
      diagnostics and convergence argument are retained in
      <code>docs/rh/rss-double-mobius-weil-completion.md</code>. The subsequent
      <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-third-splice">splice-at-1000 test</a> derives the correct cusp
      order from a new harmonic-boundary postulate, but not its coefficient or arithmetic event support. The
      <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-alternative-channels">gamma and ordinate channels</a> recover
      the exact local coefficient and sharpen the remaining problem to the fluctuating horizontal/arithmetic
      correction. The subsequent <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-ordinate-splice">two-inversion
      ordinate splice</a> resolves that correction into exact ordinate and horizontal projectors and gives its
      central value a nonnegative norm-square formula. The norm vanishes exactly under RH, but no splice or
      Möbius argument yet proves that it vanishes. The
      <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-third-density-bounce">999&ndash;666&ndash;333 bounce test</a>
      finds an exact missing-one drift rather than damping: the carry-aware motion is a 1000-state permutation.
      A strict horizontal contraction would prove RH by zero isolation, but deriving that contraction remains
      precisely the unproved step. The follow-up
      <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-golden-catchup">splice-at-one golden-delay completion</a>
      compares zeroth-seed, reindexed-Fibonacci and boundary-only readings, each with independent clocks and
      exact xi symmetries. All six tested model/schedule paths have favourable low-window zero counts, but an
      escaping-quartet stress family shows why local convergence still cannot supply the global shrinking strip.
      A further <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-internal-splices">internal-splice comparison</a>
      tests 333, 666 and 999 as extra carriers, successive Fibonacci events and three faces of one cohort.
      The <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-ternary-splices">ternary-splice test</a> separately compares
      three new carriers, three total branches and three internal states at 100, 666 and 999. The subsequent
      <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-quaternary-splice">quaternary-splice test</a> finds that
      \(F_4=3=4-1\) exactly fits the net cohort for four total branches at 1000 under full reindexing. The
      <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-splice-one-halving">splice-at-one halving test</a> proves that
      same-xi horizontal halving would be RH-equivalent, while remaining-deficit halving only accelerates the
      finite carrier limit. The <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-halving-obstruction">obstruction-closing
      attempt</a> weakens pointwise invariance to aggregate residue conservation or complete no-birth genealogy.
      The <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-global-halving-routes">four-route audit</a> then tests the prime
      ledger, global genealogy, real-rooted kernel and compactified seam routes; each retains a distinct missing
      theorem. The <a href="/riemann-splice-questions/#t-rh-final-three-routes">final-three-routes investigation</a>
